\chapter{Search Providers}
\label{providers-module}

Every academic database speaks its own language.
Semantic Scholar returns JSON with [[paperId]] fields; DBLP returns JSON with
[[@key]] identifiers in a different structure; Web of Science demands XML
responses and a completely different query syntax.
Yet from a user's perspective, these are all just sources of papers.
The challenge is hiding this heterogeneity behind a uniform interface.

This chapter implements \emph{search providers}---adapters that translate
between Scholar's internal [[Paper]] representation and each database's
native API.
A provider handles three responsibilities:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Constructing valid API requests from a query string.
\item Parsing the response format into [[Paper]] objects.
\item Managing authentication (API keys) where required.
\end{enumerate}

We use Python's [[Protocol]] type for structural typing: any object with the
right shape is a valid provider, no inheritance required.
This enables clean testing (mock objects just work), future extensibility
(third-party providers need not depend on Scholar), and explicit contracts
that static type checkers can verify.

The chapter covers fifteen providers:
\begin{description}
\item[Semantic Scholar] AI-powered research database with 200M+ papers.
  Uses the official [[semanticscholar]] client library.
\item[OpenAlex] Open catalog of 250M+ works.
  Uses the [[pyalex]] client library.
\item[DBLP] Computer science bibliography.
  Direct REST API with JSON responses.
\item[Web of Science] Comprehensive citation index.
  REST API requiring authentication.
\item[IEEE Xplore] IEEE technical literature.
  REST API requiring authentication.
\item[arXiv] Open-access preprint server with 2M+ papers.
  Uses the [[arxiv]] client library.
\item[Preprint servers] SSRN, bioRxiv, medRxiv, ChemRxiv,
  Research Square, Preprints.org, and the IACR Cryptology ePrint
  Archive---seven servers without usable search APIs of their own,
  each provided as an OpenAlex search pinned to that server
  (\cref{sec:preprint-sources}).
\item[HAL] The French national open archive.
  Direct REST API (Solr) with JSON responses.
\item[Scopus] Elsevier's abstract and citation database.
  REST API requiring authentication.
\end{description}

The preprint servers, arXiv, and HAL are also reachable collectively
through the [[preprints]] provider group (\cref{sec:provider-groups}),
which expands to all of them.

\section{Keyword metadata mapping}
\label{sec:providers-keyword-mapping}

Providers expose different kinds of term metadata.
Some preserve author-provided index terms, while others provide derived
topic labels.
Scholar models this with two distinct fields on [[Paper]]:
\begin{description}
\item[[[keywords]]] author-provided keywords or index terms.
\item[[[tags]]] provider-derived topical labels.
\end{description}

We map provider fields to these concepts as follows:
\begin{description}
\item[Semantic Scholar] [[fieldsOfStudy]] $\to$ [[tags]]
\item[OpenAlex] [[keywords]] (curated/derived) $\to$ [[tags]];
  fall back to [[concepts]] $\to$ [[tags]]
\item[IEEE Xplore] [[index_terms.author_terms]] $\to$ [[keywords]];
  [[index_terms.ieee_terms]] and [[index_terms.controlled_terms]]
  $\to$ [[tags]]
\item[Web of Science (Expanded)] [[keywords]] $\to$ [[keywords]];
  [[keywords_plus]] $\to$ [[tags]]
\item[Scopus] [[authkeywords]] $\to$ [[keywords]]
\end{description}

\section{Caching}

Each provider caches search results using [[cachetools]].
The cache is a plain dictionary keyed by the query and limit parameters.
Caches are persisted to disk using pickle, so results survive across program
runs.
This is particularly useful for systematic reviews where the same search may
be run multiple times.


