\subsection{Default providers}
\label{sec:default-providers}

Default providers are determined \emph{dynamically} based on availability.
A provider is available (and thus included as a default) if it either:
\begin{itemize}
\item Does not require an API key ([[s2]], [[openalex]],
  [[dblp]]), or
\item Requires an API key and has one configured in the environment
  ([[wos]] with [[WOS_API_KEY]], [[ieee]] with [[IEEE_API_KEY]]).
\end{itemize}

This approach ensures that users automatically get access to all providers they
can use, without manual configuration.
When a user obtains an API key for a previously unavailable provider (like
Web of Science), that provider automatically becomes a default.

Availability is not the whole story, though.
The preprint-server providers (\cref{sec:preprint-sources}) are keyless and
thus always available, but they answer through OpenAlex, so an unscoped
search would fetch the same papers twice---once through [[openalex]] and
once through each pinned server---costing extra requests just to produce
duplicates for the deduplicator to fold away.
Such providers opt out of the default set with a [[default_enabled]]
attribute set to [[False]]; they still count as configured (health checks
probe them, \cref{sec:provider-health}) and are searched whenever the user
names them explicitly.
We read the attribute with [[getattr]] and default to [[True]] so the many
providers for which the question never arises need not declare it.

<<provider registry>>=
def get_default_providers() -> list[SearchProvider]:
    """
    Get providers that are searched when the user names none.

    Returns all providers where `is_available()` returns `True`,
    except those that declare `default_enabled = False` (providers
    whose results a default search would already get elsewhere, such
    as the OpenAlex-backed preprint-server providers).
    """
    return [
        p
        for p in PROVIDERS.values()
        if p.is_available() and getattr(p, "default_enabled", True)
    ]


def get_provider_limits() -> dict[str, int | None]:
    """
    Get the maximum result limits for all registered providers.

    Returns a dict mapping provider names to their MAX_LIMIT values.
    A value of None indicates no documented maximum limit.
    """
    return {name: getattr(p, "MAX_LIMIT", None) for name, p in PROVIDERS.items()}
@

\subsubsection{Testing get\_default\_providers}

The [[get_default_providers]] function returns only providers that are
currently available.
We test this behavior:

When testing providers that require API keys, we must use [[monkeypatch]] to
remove any environment variables that might be set in the developer's
environment.
Otherwise, the test would pass or fail depending on whether the developer has
the API key configured, making the test non-reproducible.

We also need a [[# type: ignore]] comment when re-registering providers like
[[IEEEXploreProvider]] that don't implement all methods of the
[[SearchProvider]] protocol (such as [[get_paper_citations]]).
The protocol includes optional methods that not all providers support, but
Python's structural typing requires all methods to be present.
At runtime this works fine due to duck typing.

