\subsection{Provider groups}
\label{sec:provider-groups}

Once we host several preprint servers (\cref{sec:preprint-sources}), a user
running a preprint sweep would have to spell out eight [[-p]] flags.
We offer a single name, [[preprints]], that stands for all of them.

The group is an \emph{alias expanded at name-resolution time}, not a
registered meta-provider.
A meta-provider would go wrong in four ways: it would satisfy
[[is_available]] and thus join the default set, making every unscoped
search query each preprint server twice; it would show up as a bogus row in
[[scholar providers]] and its health check; it would collapse the recorded
provenance to one [[preprints]] label where review sessions need to know
\emph{which} server answered; and it would force an arbitrary answer to
whether [[--limit]] means per server or in total.
Expanding the alias into per-server names before any of that machinery runs
sidesteps all four, because downstream code only ever sees real providers.

The group deliberately includes [[arxiv]] and [[hal]] even though both are
also default providers: the point of [[preprints]] is \enquote{every
preprint server we know}, and expansion drops duplicates, so mixing the
group with explicit names stays harmless.
HAL earns its place although it archives published articles too---its
\enquote{Preprints, Working Papers} holdings are exactly what a preprint
sweep is after, and \cref{sec:hal} shows how to narrow to them.

Unknown names pass through expansion but are warned about.
[[Search.execute]] silently skips names it cannot resolve, so a typo like
[[-p openalx]] would otherwise shrink the search without a trace---the same
silent-degradation trap the health checks exist to fight
(\cref{sec:provider-health}).
Expansion is the one choke point every name passes through, so this is
where the warning belongs.

<<provider registry>>=
PROVIDER_GROUPS: dict[str, tuple[str, ...]] = {
    "preprints": (
        "arxiv",
        "hal",
        "ssrn",
        "biorxiv",
        "medrxiv",
        "chemrxiv",
        "researchsquare",
        "preprintsorg",
        "iacr",
    ),
}


def expand_provider_names(names: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Expand provider-group aliases into provider names.

    A name that matches a group in `PROVIDER_GROUPS` is replaced by the
    group's members; any other name passes through unchanged.
    Duplicates are dropped, keeping first-seen order, so mixing a group
    with explicit member names is harmless. Expansion is idempotent.

    A resulting name that is not a registered provider is kept but
    logged as a warning, since later stages skip unknown names
    silently.
    """
    expanded: list[str] = []
    for name in names:
        for member in PROVIDER_GROUPS.get(name, (name,)):
            if member not in expanded:
                expanded.append(member)
    for name in expanded:
        if name not in PROVIDERS:
            logger.warning(
                f"Unknown provider '{name}': "
                f"not a registered provider or group name."
            )
    return expanded
@

Let's verify the expansion semantics: groups expand to their registered
members, explicit names mix in without duplication, and typos warn instead
of vanishing.

