\section{Provider Health Checks}
\label{sec:provider-health}

Every provider deliberately degrades to an empty result list on error
(\cref{sec:semantic-scholar} explains why).  That is the right behaviour
\emph{during} a search---one broken provider must not sink the others---but it
is exactly the wrong behaviour \emph{before} one: an expired API key looks
identical to \enquote{no papers matched}, and a whole review can run to
completion with a silently dead provider.  This happened in practice: an
expired [[S2_API_KEY]] made Semantic Scholar answer 403 for an entire
systematic literature review, and nobody noticed until the retrieved set was
analysed (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/vt-prog-misconceptions/issues/3}).

The health check is the pre-flight counterpart: it probes each provider with a
deliberately cheap one-result request and \emph{surfaces} what the search path
hides.  Each bundled provider implements a [[check]] method returning a
[[(status, message)]] pair; [[check_provider]] wraps it with the
cross-provider logic (configuration, opt-out, network errors).  A provider
without a [[check]] method is reported as unchecked, not broken, so
third-party providers keep working.

The probe must \emph{not} reuse the provider's hardened request paths.  Those
paths exist to maximise the chance of getting data---the Semantic Scholar
provider, for instance, silently drops a rejected key and retries anonymously,
and its retrying session turns a 429 into a [[RetryError]] after
[[MAX_RETRIES]] delayed attempts.  Both behaviours mask precisely the
condition the health check
exists to report.  Each [[check]] method therefore issues one raw request with
the provider's real credentials and classifies the raw status code.

The classification distinguishes the cases a user acts on differently:
\begin{description}
\item[key rejected] 401/403 while sending a credential.  The credential is
  the problem; rotate or remove it.  This is the expired-key case.
\item[rate limited] 429.  Transient; the search path already handles this
  with back-off, so nothing is misconfigured.
\item[http error / network error] The service is unreachable or unhappy for
  reasons unrelated to credentials.
\item[ok] The probe returned data.
\end{description}
A 401/403 \emph{without} a credential cannot be a key problem, so it
classifies as an HTTP error (an IP block or outage).

<<imports>>=
import dataclasses
@

<<provider health>>=
HEALTH_OK = "ok"
HEALTH_NOT_CONFIGURED = "not configured"
HEALTH_NOT_CHECKED = "not checked"
HEALTH_KEY_REJECTED = "key rejected"
HEALTH_RATE_LIMITED = "rate limited"
HEALTH_HTTP_ERROR = "http error"
HEALTH_NETWORK_ERROR = "network error"


@dataclasses.dataclass
class ProviderHealth:
    """Outcome of probing one provider's configuration and connectivity."""

    name: str
    configured: bool
    status: str
    message: str = ""


def classify_check_response(
    response,
    *,
    authenticated: bool,
    key_hint: str = "",
) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Classify a health-probe HTTP response into a (status, message) pair.

    A 401/403 counts as a rejected key only when a credential was actually
    sent (`authenticated`); `key_hint` names the environment variable to
    check in that case.
    """
    if response.status_code in (401, 403) and authenticated:
        return HEALTH_KEY_REJECTED, (
            f"HTTP {response.status_code} -- check or replace {key_hint}"
        )
    if response.status_code == 429:
        return HEALTH_RATE_LIMITED, "HTTP 429 (transient; try again later)"
    if response.ok:
        return HEALTH_OK, ""
    return HEALTH_HTTP_ERROR, f"HTTP {response.status_code}"


def check_provider(
    provider: SearchProvider,
    live: bool = True,
) -> ProviderHealth:
    """Check one provider's configuration and, if `live`, its connectivity.

    An unconfigured provider is never probed. A provider without a `check`
    method (or with `live=False`) reports "not checked".
    """
    if not provider.is_available():
        return ProviderHealth(
            provider.name, False, HEALTH_NOT_CONFIGURED,
            "required API key missing",
        )
    if not live or not hasattr(provider, "check"):
        return ProviderHealth(provider.name, True, HEALTH_NOT_CHECKED)
    try:
        status, message = provider.check()
    except requests.RequestException as e:
        return ProviderHealth(
            provider.name, True, HEALTH_NETWORK_ERROR, str(e)
        )
    return ProviderHealth(provider.name, True, status, message)
