\subsubsection{Rate limiting and retry}

We implement two layers of rate limiting:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Proactive rate limiting}: We track the time of the last request
  and wait if necessary before making a new one.
  The interval depends on whether we have an API key.
\item \textbf{Exponential back-off}: If we still hit rate limits (HTTP 429) or
  server errors (5xx), we retry with increasing delays, up to
  [[MAX_RETRIES]] times.
\end{enumerate}

[[MAX_RETRIES]] is a named constant rather than a literal inside the [[Retry]]
because the number is also \emph{reported}: when the retries are exhausted we
tell the user how many attempts were made, and a message that disagreed with
the actual configuration would send them chasing the wrong thing.  Both
readings come from the one constant, so they cannot drift apart.

<<semantic scholar rate limiting>>=
@property
def _min_request_interval(self) -> float:
    """Return minimum interval based on whether API key is set."""
    if self.api_key and not self._key_rejected:
        return self.RATE_LIMIT_WITH_KEY
    return self.RATE_LIMIT_WITHOUT_KEY

def _create_session(self) -> requests.Session:
    """Create a session with exponential back-off retry logic."""
    session = requests.Session()
    retry = Retry(
        total=self.MAX_RETRIES,
        backoff_factor=1,  # 1s, 2s, 4s delays
        status_forcelist=[429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
        allowed_methods=["GET"],
    )
    adapter = HTTPAdapter(max_retries=retry)
    session.mount("https://", adapter)
    return session

def _wait_for_rate_limit(self) -> None:
    """Ensure minimum interval between requests."""
    elapsed = time.time() - self._last_request_time
    if elapsed < self._min_request_interval:
        time.sleep(self._min_request_interval - elapsed)
    self._last_request_time = time.time()
@

\subsubsection{Requests and the stale-key fallback}

All three endpoints (search, DOI lookup, citations/references) share the same
request preamble: wait for the rate limit, then attach the API key as an
[[x-api-key]] header.  We centralise that here so the three call sites do not
drift apart, and so a single place owns the policy for a \emph{rejected} key.

The motivating failure: an expired or revoked [[S2_API_KEY]] makes the server
answer every request with \texttt{403 Forbidden}.  This is distinct from
\texttt{429 Too Many Requests} (rate limiting): a 403 will never recover by
waiting or retrying with the same header, because the credential itself is the
problem.  Anonymous access, on the other hand, still works.  So the right
recovery is not back-off but \emph{dropping the key}: on the first 403 we warn
once, set [[_key_rejected]], and re-issue the very same request without the
header so the caller still gets data on this call.  [[_build_headers]] then
omits the key for the rest of the session, which also shifts us to the slower
anonymous rate limit (see [[_min_request_interval]] above).

If the anonymous retry \emph{also} returns 403 something more unusual is wrong
(an IP block, an outage).  We cannot recover, but we must not crash the user's
search either, so we log at [[error]] level and hand the 403 response back to
the caller, which treats it as an empty result.

<<semantic scholar request helper>>=
def _build_headers(self) -> dict:
    """Return request headers, omitting the API key once it was rejected."""
    headers = {}
    if self.api_key and not self._key_rejected:
        headers["x-api-key"] = self.api_key
    return headers

def _request(self, url: str, params: dict) -> requests.Response:
    """GET ``url`` with rate limiting and stale-key fallback.

    On a 403 while authenticated, warn once, stop sending the key for the
    rest of the session, and retry the same request anonymously. A 403 that
    persists without a key is logged at error level; the response is returned
    so the caller degrades to an empty result rather than raising.
    """
    self._wait_for_rate_limit()
    headers = self._build_headers()
    response = self._session.get(url, params=params, headers=headers, timeout=30)

    if response.status_code == 403 and "x-api-key" in headers:
        logger.warning(
            "s2: API key rejected (403) -- check or replace S2_API_KEY; "
            "falling back to anonymous access"
        )
        self._key_rejected = True
        self._wait_for_rate_limit()
        response = self._session.get(
            url, params=params, headers=self._build_headers(), timeout=30
        )

    if response.status_code == 403:
        logger.error(
            "s2: Forbidden (403) even without an API key; "
            "returning no results for this request"
        )

    return response
@

\subsubsection{Health check}

The health probe must bypass both resilience layers above.  [[_request]]'s
stale-key fallback silently drops a rejected key and retries
anonymously---exactly the condition the health check exists to surface---and
[[_session]]'s [[Retry]] turns a 429 into a [[RetryError]] after
[[MAX_RETRIES]] delayed attempts, which would misreport plain rate limiting
as a network error.  So the
probe issues one raw [[requests.get]] with the configured key attached and
lets [[classify_check_response]] read the raw status code
(\cref{sec:provider-health}).

<<semantic scholar check method>>=
def check(self) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Probe the search endpoint with a one-result query.

    Sends the configured API key (if any) so a stale key is surfaced
    rather than silently dropped.
    """
    self._wait_for_rate_limit()
    headers = {"x-api-key": self.api_key} if self.api_key else {}
    response = requests.get(
        self.API_URL,
        params={"query": "test", "limit": 1},
        headers=headers,
        timeout=30,
    )
    return classify_check_response(
        response,
        authenticated=bool(self.api_key),
        key_hint="S2_API_KEY",
    )
@

\subsubsection{Search method}

The search method calls the API with rate limiting and handles errors.
When the user requests more results than a single API call can return (the
Semantic Scholar API caps at 100 results per request), we automatically fetch
multiple pages using the [[offset]] parameter.

This pagination is transparent to the caller: they simply request the number of
results they want, and the provider handles the underlying paging.
The loop terminates when we have fetched enough results, when the API returns
no more data, or when we reach the total number of available results.

As with DBLP, [[search]] caches only \emph{trustworthy} results.  A rate
limit, a rejected key, or an HTTP/parse error can cut a search short, and the
persistent pickle cache would otherwise freeze that partial or empty result for
every later call with the same arguments.  We therefore split the work: the
inner [[_search_uncached]] reports a [[cacheable]] flag alongside its papers,
and the thin [[search]] wrapper writes to the cache only when that flag is
true.  Partial results are still \emph{returned} to the caller---the user sees
what we managed to fetch---they are just not cached.

<<semantic scholar search method>>=
def search(
    self,
    query: str,
    limit: int = 100,
    filters: SearchFilters | None = None,
) -> list[Paper]:
    """
    Search Semantic Scholar for papers matching the query.

    Automatically fetches multiple pages if the requested limit exceeds
    the API's per-request maximum (100 results per page).
    """
    cache_key = (query, limit, filters.cache_key() if filters else "")
    if cache_key in self._cache:
        return self._cache[cache_key]

    papers, cacheable = self._search_uncached(query, limit, filters)
    if cacheable:
        self._cache[cache_key] = papers
    return papers

def _search_uncached(
    self,
    query: str,
    limit: int,
    filters: SearchFilters | None,
) -> tuple[list[Paper], bool]:
    """Run the paged search, returning ``(papers, cacheable)``.

    ``cacheable`` is False when a transient failure (rate limit, rejected
    key, HTTP or parse error) truncated the search, so the caller can avoid
    freezing a partial or empty result into the cache.
    """
    all_papers: list[Paper] = []
    offset = 0
    page_size = min(limit, 100)  # S2 API returns max 100 per request

    try:
        while len(all_papers) < limit:
            # Calculate how many results we still need
            remaining = limit - len(all_papers)
            current_page_size = min(page_size, remaining)

            params = {
                "query": query,
                "limit": current_page_size,
                "offset": offset,
                "fields": "title,authors,year,abstract,venue,externalIds,url,openAccessPdf,citationCount,publicationTypes,fieldsOfStudy",
            }

            # Apply filters (Semantic Scholar supports most filters natively)
            <<semantic scholar apply filters>>

            response = self._request(self.API_URL, params)

            if response.status_code == 429:
                logger.warning(
                    "s2: Rate limited (429). "
                    "Get a free API key at "
                    "https://www.semanticscholar.org/product/api#api-key-form "
                    "and set S2_API_KEY environment variable."
                )
                return all_papers, False

            if response.status_code == 403:
                # Key rejected and anonymous access also forbidden; the
                # helper already logged the error. Don't cache the empty.
                return all_papers, False

            response.raise_for_status()
            data = response.json()

            papers = data.get("data", [])
            if not papers:
                # No more results available
                break

            all_papers.extend(self._convert_paper(p) for p in papers)
            offset += len(papers)

            # Check if we've fetched all available results
            total = data.get("total", 0)
            if offset >= total:
                break

        logger.debug(f"semantic_scholar: Retrieved {len(all_papers)} papers")
        return all_papers, True
    except requests.exceptions.RetryError:
        logger.warning(
            "s2: Rate limited (429) after %d retries. "
            "Get a free API key at "
            "https://www.semanticscholar.org/product/api#api-key-form "
            "and set S2_API_KEY environment variable.",
            self.MAX_RETRIES,
        )
        return all_papers, False
    except requests.exceptions.HTTPError as e:
        logger.warning("s2: HTTP error: %s", e)
        return all_papers, False
    except Exception as e:
        logger.warning("s2: %s", e)
        return all_papers, False
@

