\section{OpenAlex Provider}
\label{sec:openalex}

OpenAlex is a free and open catalog of the world's scholarly works, authors,
venues, institutions, and concepts.
It covers over 250 million works and is fully open access.

\subsection{Package Choice}

We use the [[pyalex]] Python package which provides a clean interface to the
OpenAlex API. Unlike Semantic Scholar's package, [[pyalex]] returns raw
dictionaries rather than typed objects, requiring manual field extraction.

\subsection{Authentication}

No API key is required, but providing an email address gives access to the
\enquote{polite pool}---a separate pool of API resources with faster response
times for identified users.  The address comes from the shared
[[contact_email]] resolver (\cref{sec:contact-email}): the generic
[[SCHOLAR_EMAIL]], or [[OPENALEX_EMAIL]] as a service-specific override.

This is OpenAlex's approach to encouraging good API citizenship without
creating barriers to access.

\subsection{Rate Limiting and Pagination}

OpenAlex allows up to 10 requests per second.
We implement proactive rate limiting by tracking the time of the last request
and waiting if necessary before making a new one.
This ensures we stay well within limits even during batch operations like
paper enrichment.

The timestamp lives at module level, not on the provider instance.
The request budget belongs to the \emph{client} as seen by OpenAlex, and
several provider objects speak to that one API: this class plus every
source-pinned subclass (\cref{sec:preprint-sources}).
Per-instance timestamps would each start at zero, letting a
[[-p preprints]] search fire one immediate request per provider; the shared
timestamp makes them take turns.

<<openalex constants>>=
_openalex_last_request: float = 0.0
@

The [[pyalex]] library provides a convenient [[paginate()]] method that
automatically handles pagination.
When the user requests more results than a single API page can return (200
results per page), we iterate through the paginated results until we reach
the requested limit.
This makes large result sets transparent to the caller.

\subsection{Caching only successful searches}

Search results are cached manually rather than with [[@cachedmethod]].
The decorator caches whatever the method returns, including the empty list
our error handling produces when OpenAlex is briefly unreachable---after
which the failure is frozen and every retry of that query silently
\enquote{succeeds} with zero results until the cache is cleared.
This is the same trap documented for DBLP
(\cref{sec:dblp-transient-failures}), and it grows with every
source-pinned subclass that inherits this method.
Splitting the work into an uncached worker and a caching wrapper lets the
wrapper store only what the worker returns without raising: a genuine
zero-hit answer is still cached (it is a real answer), a failure never is.

\subsection{Provider implementation}

<<openalex provider>>=
class OpenAlexProvider:
    """Search provider for OpenAlex."""

    name = "openalex"
    MAX_LIMIT: int | None = None   # No documented maximum
    RATE_LIMIT = 0.1               # 10 requests/second = 0.1 seconds between requests
    CACHE_VERSION = 2              # Invalidate persisted caches when changing parsing

    def __init__(self, email: str | None = None):
        """
        Initialize the OpenAlex provider.

        Args:
            email: Optional email for polite pool access (faster responses).
                   If not provided, uses the OPENALEX_EMAIL or SCHOLAR_EMAIL
                   environment variable.
        """
        configured_email = email or contact_email("OPENALEX_EMAIL")
        if configured_email:
            pyalex.config.email = configured_email
        self._cache: dict = load_cache(self.name)
        register_cache(self.name, self._cache)

    def is_available(self) -> bool:
        """OpenAlex is always available (email is optional)."""
        return True

    def _wait_for_rate_limit(self) -> None:
        """Ensure minimum interval between OpenAlex requests."""
        global _openalex_last_request
        elapsed = time.time() - _openalex_last_request
        if elapsed < self.RATE_LIMIT:
            time.sleep(self.RATE_LIMIT - elapsed)
        _openalex_last_request = time.time()

    def search(
        self,
        query: str,
        limit: int = 100,
        filters: SearchFilters | None = None,
    ) -> list[Paper]:
        """
        Search OpenAlex for papers matching the query.

        Automatically fetches multiple pages if the requested limit exceeds
        the API's per-request maximum (200 results per page).
        """
        cache_key = (
            self.CACHE_VERSION,
            query,
            limit,
            filters.cache_key() if filters else "",
        )
        if cache_key in self._cache:
            return self._cache[cache_key]
        try:
            papers = self._search_uncached(query, limit, filters)
        except Exception as e:
            logger.warning("%s: %s", self.name, e)
            return []
        self._cache[cache_key] = papers
        return papers

    def _search_uncached(
        self,
        query: str,
        limit: int,
        filters: SearchFilters | None,
    ) -> list[Paper]:
        """Fetch papers from OpenAlex without touching the cache."""
        all_papers: list[Paper] = []
        page_size = min(limit, 200)  # OpenAlex returns max 200 per page

        logger.debug(f"{self.name}: Searching for '{query}' with limit={limit}")
        self._wait_for_rate_limit()
        works = self._base_query(query)
        self._warn_about_filters(filters)

        # Apply filters (OpenAlex supports most filters natively)
        <<openalex apply filters>>

        # Use pagination to fetch all results up to the limit
        for page in works.paginate(per_page=page_size):
            self._wait_for_rate_limit()
            for work in page:
                all_papers.append(self._convert_work(work))
                if len(all_papers) >= limit:
                    logger.debug(f"{self.name}: Retrieved {len(all_papers)} papers")
                    return all_papers

        logger.debug(f"{self.name}: Retrieved {len(all_papers)} papers")
        return all_papers

