\subsubsection{Extension seam for source-pinned providers}
\label{sec:openalex-extension-seam}

The preprint-server providers (\cref{sec:preprint-sources}) are OpenAlex
searches narrowed to one source.
Everything else---pagination, rate limiting, caching, filter translation,
work conversion---is identical, so they subclass this provider rather than
duplicate it.
Two methods form the seam.
[[_base_query]] produces the query builder that the filters then narrow;
a subclass overrides it to attach its source filter, and because [[pyalex]]
merges repeated [[filter()]] calls additively, the user's filters compose
with the pin instead of replacing it.
[[_warn_about_filters]] runs before the search so a subclass can flag
filter combinations that are technically valid but almost certainly not
what the user meant (for example a venue filter on a provider already
pinned to one venue); the base class has nothing to warn about.

<<openalex extension hooks>>=
def _base_query(self, query: str):
    """Return the pyalex query builder for `query`."""
    return pyalex.Works().search(query)

def _warn_about_filters(self, filters: SearchFilters | None) -> None:
    """Warn about filters this provider handles surprisingly.

    The base provider handles all filters natively; subclasses
    override this to log warnings for filter combinations that
    interact badly with their source pinning.
    """
@

\subsubsection{Converting work format}

OpenAlex returns works as dictionaries.
We extract the relevant fields and convert to our [[Paper]] type:

<<convert openalex work>>=
def _convert_work(self, work: dict) -> Paper:
    """Convert an OpenAlex work to our Paper type."""
    # Extract author names from authorships
    authors = []
    for authorship in work.get("authorships", []):
        author_info = authorship.get("author", {})
        name = author_info.get("display_name")
        if name:
            authors.append(name)

    # Extract DOI (remove URL prefix if present)
    doi = work.get("doi")
    if doi and doi.startswith("https://doi.org/"):
        doi = doi[16:]

    # Extract venue from primary location
    venue = None
    primary_location = work.get("primary_location", {})
    if primary_location:
        source = primary_location.get("source", {})
        if source:
            venue = source.get("display_name")

    # Extract PDF URL from open_access or primary_location
    pdf_url = None
    open_access = work.get("open_access", {})
    if open_access:
        pdf_url = open_access.get("oa_url")
    if not pdf_url and primary_location:
        pdf_url = primary_location.get("pdf_url")

    # Abstract: OpenAlex typically provides abstract_inverted_index.
    abstract = work.get("abstract")
    if not abstract:
        inverted = work.get("abstract_inverted_index")
        if isinstance(inverted, dict) and inverted:
            abstract = self._reconstruct_abstract(inverted)

    # Derive arXiv DOI from arXiv URLs if OpenAlex lacks a DOI.
    if not doi:
        doi = (
            arxiv_doi_from_url(pdf_url)
            or arxiv_doi_from_url(
                open_access.get("oa_url") if open_access else None
            )
            or arxiv_doi_from_url(
                primary_location.get("landing_page_url")
                if primary_location
                else None
            )
        )

    tags_list: list[str] = []
    for kw in work.get("keywords", []) or []:
        if isinstance(kw, dict):
            name = kw.get("display_name")
            if name:
                tags_list.append(name)
        elif isinstance(kw, str) and kw:
            tags_list.append(kw)
    if not tags_list:
        for concept in work.get("concepts", []) or []:
            if isinstance(concept, dict):
                name = concept.get("display_name")
                if name:
                    tags_list.append(name)
    tags = tags_list or None

    return Paper(
        title=work.get("title", ""),
        authors=authors,
        year=work.get("publication_year"),
        doi=doi,
        abstract=abstract,
        venue=venue,
        url=work.get("id"),
        pdf_url=pdf_url,
        tags=tags,
        sources=[self.name],
    )


def _reconstruct_abstract(
    self,
    inverted_index: dict[str, list[int]],
) -> str | None:
    """Reconstruct abstract text from an OpenAlex inverted index."""
    try:
        max_pos = max(
            max(positions)
            for positions in inverted_index.values()
            if isinstance(positions, list) and positions
        )
    except ValueError:
        return None

    words: list[str] = [""] * (max_pos + 1)
    for word, positions in inverted_index.items():
        if not isinstance(word, str) or not isinstance(positions, list):
            continue
        for pos in positions:
            if isinstance(pos, int) and 0 <= pos <= max_pos:
                words[pos] = word

    abstract = " ".join(w for w in words if w).strip()
    return abstract or None
@

\subsubsection{DOI lookup}

Like Semantic Scholar, we can fetch a single paper by its DOI.
OpenAlex uses DOI URLs as identifiers, so we query by the full DOI URL.

As with the Semantic Scholar implementation, we accept [[str | None]] to
simplify calling code that passes [[paper.doi]] without checking for [[None]]
first.

A 404 is an \emph{answer}, not an error: OpenAlex simply has no work
under that DOI.  This is routine rather than exceptional---arXiv DOIs
in particular are often absent even when the work itself is indexed
(under a merged record without the arXiv DOI)---and the preprint
fallback (\cref{preprint-module}) probes candidate DOIs exactly like
that on every enrichment.  So we cache the miss like a successful
lookup and log at debug level; treating it as a warning turned every
enrich run into a wall of noise, and leaving it uncached re-asked the
same dead DOI on every run.  Other HTTP errors and transport failures
stay uncached warnings, so transient trouble is retried later.

<<openalex get paper by doi>>=
def get_paper_by_doi(self, doi: str | None) -> Paper | None:
    """
    Fetch a single paper by its DOI.

    Args:
        doi: The DOI to look up (e.g., "10.1145/1234567.1234568").

    Returns:
        Paper if found, None otherwise.
    """
    if not doi:
        return None

    # Check cache first
    cache_key = ("doi", self.CACHE_VERSION, doi)
    if cache_key in self._cache:
        logger.info(f"openalex: Cache hit for DOI {doi}")
        return self._cache[cache_key]

    logger.info(f"openalex: Cache miss for DOI {doi}, fetching from API")

    try:
        self._wait_for_rate_limit()

        # OpenAlex uses full DOI URL as identifier
        doi_url = f"https://doi.org/{doi}"
        work = pyalex.Works()[doi_url]

        if not work:
            self._cache[cache_key] = None
            return None

        paper = self._convert_work(work)
        self._cache[cache_key] = paper
        return paper

    except requests.exceptions.HTTPError as e:
        if getattr(e.response, "status_code", None) == 404:
            # DOI unknown to OpenAlex: cache the definite miss.
            logger.debug("openalex: no work for DOI %s", doi)
            self._cache[cache_key] = None
            return None
        logger.warning("openalex: DOI lookup failed: %s", e)
        return None
    except Exception as e:
        logger.warning("openalex: DOI lookup failed: %s", e)
        return None
@

\subsubsection{Preprint locations of a work}

An OpenAlex work carries a [[locations]] array listing \emph{every}
host of the work---publisher, aggregators, and repository deposits.
[[_convert_work]] deliberately reads only [[primary_location]] and
[[open_access]], but for the preprint fallback
(\cref{preprint-module}) the rest of the array is the payload: when a
preprint deposit was merged into the same OpenAlex work, it appears
here as a location whose source is a known preprint server, complete
with its own PDF link.  Reading the array costs nothing extra---it is
in the same response the DOI lookup already fetches.

A location counts as a preprint host in either of two ways: its
source ID matches a server in [[PREPRINT_SOURCES]], or its URLs point
into arXiv.  The second test is needed because arXiv is not in
[[PREPRINT_SOURCES]] (it has a native provider), yet it is by far the
most common repository location.  Source IDs in location objects are
full URLs ([[https://openalex.org/S4210172589]]) while
[[PREPRINT_SOURCES]] stores bare IDs, so we compare the URL's tail.

The result contract mirrors [[fetch_preprint_dois]] in the Crossref
module: an empty list for both \enquote{no preprint host} and
\enquote{lookup failed}, cached only in the former case so transient
failures retry.

<<openalex preprint lookups>>=
def get_preprint_locations(self, doi: str | None) -> list[dict]:
    """Return preprint-server locations hosting the work `doi`.

    Each location dict has `source_id`, `source_name`, `pdf_url`,
    `landing_page_url`, and `is_oa`. Returns an empty list when the
    work has no preprint-server location or the lookup fails.
    """
    if not doi:
        return []

    cache_key = ("preprint_locations", self.CACHE_VERSION, doi)
    if cache_key in self._cache:
        return self._cache[cache_key]

    try:
        self._wait_for_rate_limit()
        doi_url = f"https://doi.org/{doi}"
        work = pyalex.Works()[doi_url]
    except requests.exceptions.HTTPError as e:
        if getattr(e.response, "status_code", None) == 404:
            # DOI unknown to OpenAlex: cache the definite miss.
            logger.debug("openalex: no work for DOI %s", doi)
            self._cache[cache_key] = []
            return []
        logger.warning(
            "openalex: preprint-location lookup failed for %s: %s",
            doi, e,
        )
        return []
    except Exception as e:
        logger.warning(
            "openalex: preprint-location lookup failed for %s: %s",
            doi, e,
        )
        return []

    locations = self._preprint_locations_from_work(work or {})
    self._cache[cache_key] = locations
    return locations

def _preprint_locations_from_work(self, work: dict) -> list[dict]:
    """Filter a work's locations down to preprint-server hosts."""
    known_ids = {source_id for _name, source_id, _label in PREPRINT_SOURCES}
    found = []
    for location in work.get("locations") or []:
        source = location.get("source") or {}
        source_id = (source.get("id") or "").rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        pdf_url = location.get("pdf_url")
        landing_page_url = location.get("landing_page_url")
        hosted_on_server = source_id in known_ids
        hosted_on_arxiv = bool(
            arxiv_doi_from_url(pdf_url)
            or arxiv_doi_from_url(landing_page_url)
        )
        if not (hosted_on_server or hosted_on_arxiv):
            continue
        found.append(
            {
                "source_id": source_id or None,
                "source_name": source.get("display_name"),
                "pdf_url": pdf_url,
                "landing_page_url": landing_page_url,
                "is_oa": location.get("is_oa"),
            }
        )
    return found
@

\subsubsection{Title search over preprint works}

The search tier of the preprint fallback needs \enquote{preprints with
this title} across all servers.  Fanning out to the nine providers of
the [[preprints]] group would cost nine requests per paper; but the
seven [[PREPRINT_SOURCES]] servers are OpenAlex sources anyway, and
OpenAlex classifies works with [[type="preprint"]], so \emph{one}
type-filtered title search spans them all (plus arXiv and HAL, which
OpenAlex also indexes).  Conversion reuses [[_convert_work]], which
already derives canonical arXiv DOIs from arXiv URLs---exactly what
the caller needs to adopt a hit as a citable entry.

We search [[title.search]] rather than the general [[search]] field:
the caller matches candidates on exact normalized title, so hits whose
title merely \emph{mentions} the query words are useless and abstracts
would only add noise.

Real titles routinely contain characters that are syntax in an
OpenAlex filter expression, so the query is sanitized first.  A [[?]]
or [[*]] makes OpenAlex treat the query as a wildcard search, which
the stemmed [[title.search]] field rejects outright (any title ending
in a question mark failed).  A comma separates filter clauses and a
pipe separates OR alternatives, so either inside a title truncates or
corrupts the filter (a 400 for titles like \enquote{RepairAgent: An
Autonomous, LLM-Based Agent \dots}).  Replacing all four with spaces
is lossless for our purpose: the search is stemmed, so punctuation
carries no signal, and the caller re-checks candidates against the
\emph{original} title anyway.

<<openalex preprint lookups>>=
@staticmethod
def _sanitize_title_query(title: str) -> str:
    """Strip characters that are OpenAlex filter/wildcard syntax."""
    cleaned = re.sub(r"[*?,|]", " ", title)
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", cleaned).strip()

def search_preprints_by_title(
    self, title: str, limit: int = 5
) -> list[Paper]:
    """Search OpenAlex for preprint works matching `title`.

    Filters on OpenAlex's work type `preprint`, which covers the
    dedicated preprint servers as well as arXiv and HAL deposits.
    Returns an empty list on failure.
    """
    if not title or not title.strip():
        return []

    normalized = self._sanitize_title_query(title)
    if not normalized:
        return []
    cache_key = (
        "preprint_search", self.CACHE_VERSION, normalized.lower(),
    )
    if cache_key in self._cache:
        return self._cache[cache_key]

    try:
        self._wait_for_rate_limit()
        works = (
            pyalex.Works()
            .filter(title={"search": normalized}, type="preprint")
            .get(per_page=limit)
        )
    except Exception as e:
        logger.warning(
            "openalex: preprint title search failed: %s", e
        )
        return []

    papers = [self._convert_work(work) for work in works or []]
    self._cache[cache_key] = papers
    return papers
@

