\subsubsection{Health check}

The probe targets whichever API tier initialisation selected, mirroring that
tier's parameter format (the two APIs share nothing but the [[X-ApiKey]]
header).  Since both tiers require a key, any 401/403 here means the key is
the problem; the hint names both environment variables because either could
be the one that was set.

<<wos check method>>=
def check(self) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Probe the active WoS API tier with a one-result query."""
    if self._api_tier == "expanded":
        params = {
            "databaseId": "WOS",
            "usrQuery": "TS=(test)",
            "count": 1,
            "firstRecord": 1,
        }
    else:
        params = {"q": "TS=(test)", "db": "WOS", "limit": 1, "page": 1}
    response = requests.get(
        self._api_url,
        params=params,
        headers={"X-ApiKey": self._api_key},
        timeout=30,
    )
    return classify_check_response(
        response,
        authenticated=True,
        key_hint="WOS_EXPANDED_API_KEY or WOS_STARTER_API_KEY",
    )
