\section{Preprint Server Providers}
\label{sec:preprint-sources}

arXiv serves physics, mathematics, and computer science; most other
disciplines grew their own preprint servers.
This section adds providers for seven of them: SSRN (social science,
economics, law, finance), bioRxiv (life sciences), medRxiv (medicine),
ChemRxiv (chemistry), Research Square, Preprints.org (both
multidisciplinary), and the IACR Cryptology ePrint Archive (cryptology).

\subsection{Why OpenAlex instead of the native APIs}

Unlike arXiv, none of these servers offers an API we can search
(investigated 2026-08-26):
\begin{description}
\item[SSRN, Research Square] publish no public search API at all
  (Research Square gates API access behind an approval form).
\item[bioRxiv, medRxiv] offer an API, but it only serves metadata for a
  given DOI or date range---there is no keyword search.
\item[ChemRxiv] documents a search API, but Cloudflare's bot protection
  blocks non-browser clients, so a [[requests]]-based provider cannot
  reach it.
\item[Preprints.org] gates its API behind a registration form.
\item[IACR ePrint] offers only an HTML search (whose pages carry a
  text-and-data-mining reservation) and a harvest-only OAI-PMH endpoint,
  neither usable for keyword queries.
\end{description}

OpenAlex, however, indexes all seven as \emph{sources}, typically within
days of publication, and its filter syntax can pin a search to one source.
So each provider here is simply the OpenAlex provider narrowed to one
source ID---the entire conversion, pagination, caching, and rate-limiting
machinery of \cref{sec:openalex} is inherited through the extension seam
(\cref{sec:openalex-extension-seam}).

Two trade-offs come with this route, and both matter when these providers
feed a systematic review protocol.
First, searching a server through OpenAlex is not the same as searching
the server's own website: OpenAlex matches against its own record of
title, abstract, and full text, so result sets will differ.
Second, OpenAlex's coverage of a server is not guaranteed complete; for
the IACR archive it holds roughly 14\,000 of some 25\,000 papers.
Record the provider name, not the server's website, as the search
provenance.

We pin sources with the [[locations.source.id]] filter rather than
[[primary_location.source.id]].
A preprint that is later published elsewhere keeps the preprint server
only as a non-primary location---pinning on the primary location would
hide exactly the preprints that went on to be published.
The difference is large: for the IACR archive, 13\,723 works carry it as
\emph{a} location but only 7\,274 as their \emph{primary} one.

TechRxiv (IEEE's preprint server) is the known gap: it has no public API
\emph{and} is absent from OpenAlex, so there is currently no route to it
at all.

\subsection{The provider class}

The class narrows [[OpenAlexProvider]] through the two hooks of
\cref{sec:openalex-extension-seam} and parameterises everything
server-specific---name, source ID, human-readable label---so one class
serves all seven servers.
Assigning [[self.name]] \emph{before} calling [[super().__init__()]] is
load-bearing: the parent constructor names the persistent cache after
[[self.name]], so each server gets its own cache file instead of
poisoning the shared OpenAlex one (the cache key does not include the
source ID).
For the same reason each instance's papers are stamped with the server's
own name in [[sources]], which is what makes per-server provenance and
cross-provider deduplication work downstream.

These providers set [[default_enabled]] to [[False]]
(\cref{sec:default-providers}): their answers are a subset of what the
plain [[openalex]] provider already returns, so an unscoped search gains
nothing from them but duplicate requests.
They participate only when named explicitly---directly or through the
[[preprints]] group (\cref{sec:provider-groups}).

<<preprint source provider>>=
class OpenAlexSourceProvider(OpenAlexProvider):
    """OpenAlex search pinned to a single preprint server."""

    default_enabled = False

    def __init__(
        self,
        name: str,
        source_id: str,
        display_name: str,
        email: str | None = None,
    ):
        """
        Initialize a source-pinned OpenAlex provider.

        Args:
            name: Provider name, also names the persistent cache.
            source_id: OpenAlex source ID (e.g. "S4210172589").
            display_name: Human-readable server name for messages.
            email: Optional email for polite pool access.
        """
        self.name = name
        self.source_id = source_id
        self.display_name = display_name
        super().__init__(email=email)

    def _base_query(self, query: str):
        """Return the pyalex query builder pinned to this source."""
        return super()._base_query(query).filter(
            locations={"source": {"id": self.source_id}}
        )

    <<preprint source filter warnings>>
    <<preprint source check method>>
@

\subsubsection{Filter warnings}

All filters still work---[[pyalex]] merges repeated [[filter()]] calls,
so the user's filters compose with the source pin---but two combinations
deserve a warning.
A venue filter ANDs a venue name with a source pin that \emph{is} a
venue, which almost always yields zero results.
A publication-type filter that excludes preprints runs against servers
whose holdings are almost entirely preprints; it is not wrong (a work
published after preprinting can carry another type), but it will discard
most of what the server has, so the user should hear about it.
This mirrors how the arXiv provider warns about non-preprint type filters
(\cref{sec:arxiv}).

<<preprint source filter warnings>>=
def _warn_about_filters(self, filters: SearchFilters | None) -> None:
    """Warn about filters that clash with the source pinning."""
    if not filters:
        return
    if filters.venue:
        logger.warning(
            f"{self.name}: results are already limited to "
            f"{self.display_name}; a venue filter on top of that "
            f"almost always yields nothing."
        )
    if filters.pub_types and "preprint" not in [
        t.lower() for t in filters.pub_types
    ]:
        logger.warning(
            f"{self.name}: {self.display_name} hosts almost only "
            f"preprints; filtering by types {filters.pub_types} will "
            f"discard most results."
        )
@

