\section{HAL Provider}
\label{sec:hal}

HAL (\emph{Hyper Articles en Ligne}) is the French national open archive:
multidisciplinary, strong in mathematics, computer science, physics, and
the humanities, and---unlike the servers of
\cref{sec:preprint-sources}---equipped with a real search API.
Its \enquote{Preprints, Working Papers} holdings make it a natural member
of the [[preprints]] group (\cref{sec:provider-groups}), though it also
archives published articles; narrowing to preprints only is a matter of
[[--type preprint]] (see the type mapping below).

\subsection{API access}

The API is a thinly wrapped Solr instance: no key, no registration,
standard Solr query syntax including [[AND]]/[[OR]]/[[NOT]], quoted
phrases, and parentheses.
That is the same boolean syntax users write for other providers, and HAL
\emph{rejects} what it cannot parse rather than silently mis-searching, so
unlike DBLP (\cref{sec:dblp-sanitize}) the query is sent verbatim: there
is nothing to sanitise, and rewriting would only corrupt the recorded
provenance.

Two probed behaviours (2026-08-26) shape the implementation:
\begin{description}
\item[The field list is mandatory] Without an explicit [[fl]] parameter
  the response carries only [[docid]], [[label_s]], and [[uri_s]]---no
  title, authors, abstract, or DOI.
  Every request therefore names the fields it needs.
  Several of those come back as \emph{lists} ([[title_s]],
  [[authFullName_s]], [[abstract_s]], [[keyword_s]]); conversion unwraps
  them.
\item[Errors hide behind HTTP 200] A malformed Solr query (a bare colon,
  an unbalanced quote) is answered with status 200 and an [[error]]
  object in the JSON body, so [[raise_for_status]] passes and only body
  inspection catches it.
  Missing that would cache the failure as an empty result---the frozen
  cache-entry trap of \cref{sec:dblp-transient-failures} all over again.
\end{description}

\subsection{Provider implementation}

The class mirrors the DBLP provider's shape---manual caching, a paging
loop over a single-page fetcher, and an internal exception separating
transient or rejected requests from genuine zero-hit answers.
[[requests]] is already imported for the other REST providers, so no new
dependencies are needed.

<<hal provider>>=
class _HALUnavailable(Exception):
    """Raised internally when HAL rejects a query or fails transiently.

    Used to distinguish a rejected query or unusable response (do not
    cache, allow a retry later) from a genuine zero-result answer
    (safe to cache).
    """


class HALProvider:
    """Search provider for HAL, the French open archive."""

    name = "hal"
    MAX_LIMIT: int | None = None   # Deep paging verified past start=30000

    def __init__(self):
        """Initialize the HAL provider."""
        self._cache: dict = load_cache(self.name)
        register_cache(self.name, self._cache)

    def is_available(self) -> bool:
        """HAL is always available (no API key needed)."""
        return True

