\section{Scopus Provider}

Scopus is Elsevier's abstract and citation database, covering over
27\,000 journals across all disciplines.
Unlike Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex, Scopus requires an API key
obtained from \url{https://dev.elsevier.com/}.
An optional institutional token extends access beyond the standard
rate limits.

The Scopus Search API differs from other providers in two notable ways:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Filters are embedded in the query string using Scopus's own syntax
  (e.g.\ [[PUBYEAR = 2024]]) rather than passed as separate parameters.
\item The API returns at most 25 results per page---significantly lower
  than IEEE's 200 or DBLP's 1000---so pagination is essential even for
  modest limits.
\end{enumerate}

A limitation of the Search API is that it only returns the first author
([[dc:creator]]).
Full author lists require a separate Abstract Retrieval API call, which
we omit here to keep the provider simple and avoid extra API requests.

\subsection{Error handling}

The Scopus API uses standard HTTP status codes.
We provide actionable messages for the most common failure modes:
authentication errors (401), permission issues (403), and rate limiting
(429).

<<scopus handle response errors>>=
if response.status_code != 200:
    if response.status_code == 401:
        logger.warning(
            "scopus: Authentication failed (HTTP 401). "
            "Check your SCOPUS_API_KEY at "
            "https://dev.elsevier.com/"
        )
    elif response.status_code == 429:
        logger.warning(
            "scopus: Rate limited (HTTP 429). "
            "Wait before making more requests."
        )
    elif response.status_code == 403:
        logger.warning(
            "scopus: Access denied (HTTP 403). "
            "Your API key may lack Scopus Search "
            "permissions."
        )
    else:
        logger.warning(
            "scopus: API error (HTTP %d): %s",
            response.status_code,
            response.text[:200],
        )
    response.raise_for_status()
@

\subsection{Provider implementation}

Authentication uses HTTP headers rather than query parameters---the
[[X-ELS-APIKey]] header carries the API key and [[X-ELS-Insttoken]]
carries the optional institutional token.

The pagination loop uses 0-indexed [[start]] and [[count]] parameters.
Scopus signals empty results by returning an entry with
[[@_fa]] set to [["false"]] rather than an empty array, so we check
for this sentinel.

<<scopus provider>>=
class ScopusProvider:
    """Search provider for Elsevier Scopus."""

    name = "scopus"
    MAX_LIMIT = 25  # Scopus returns max 25 per page

    def __init__(
        self,
        api_key: str | None = None,
        inst_token: str | None = None,
    ):
        """Initialize the Scopus provider.

        Args:
            api_key: API key for Scopus API. Falls back
                to SCOPUS_API_KEY environment variable.
            inst_token: Institutional token for extended
                access. Falls back to SCOPUS_INST_TOKEN.
        """
        self.api_key = (
            api_key or os.environ.get("SCOPUS_API_KEY")
        )
        self.inst_token = (
            inst_token
            or os.environ.get("SCOPUS_INST_TOKEN")
        )
        self._cache: dict = load_cache(self.name)
        register_cache(self.name, self._cache)

    def is_available(self) -> bool:
        """Scopus requires an API key."""
        return bool(self.api_key)

    @cachedmethod(
        lambda self: self._cache,
        key=lambda self, query, limit=100, filters=None: (
            query,
            limit,
            filters.cache_key() if filters else "",
        ),
    )
    def search(
        self,
        query: str,
        limit: int = 100,
        filters: SearchFilters | None = None,
    ) -> list[Paper]:
        """Search Scopus for papers matching the query.

        Fetches multiple pages when the requested limit
        exceeds the per-request maximum of 25 results.
        """
        if not self.api_key:
            return []

        logger.debug(
            "scopus: Searching for '%s' with limit=%d",
            query, limit,
        )

        search_query = query
        <<scopus apply filters>>

        all_papers: list[Paper] = []
        start = 0
        page_size = self.MAX_LIMIT

        try:
            while len(all_papers) < limit:
                remaining = limit - len(all_papers)
                current_count = min(page_size, remaining)

                headers = {
                    "X-ELS-APIKey": self.api_key,
                    "Accept": "application/json",
                }
                if self.inst_token:
                    headers["X-ELS-Insttoken"] = (
                        self.inst_token
                    )

                params = {
                    "query": search_query,
                    "start": start,
                    "count": current_count,
                }

                response = requests.get(
                    SCOPUS_API_URL,
                    headers=headers,
                    params=params,
                    timeout=30,
                )
                <<scopus handle response errors>>
                data = response.json()

                results = data.get("search-results", {})
                entries = results.get("entry", [])

                if not entries or (
                    len(entries) == 1
                    and entries[0].get("@_fa") == "false"
                ):
                    break

                all_papers.extend(
                    self._convert_entry(entry)
                    for entry in entries
                    if entry.get("@_fa") != "false"
                )
                start += len(entries)

                total = int(
                    results.get(
                        "opensearch:totalResults", 0
                    )
                )
                if start >= total:
                    break

            logger.debug(
                "scopus: Retrieved %d papers",
                len(all_papers),
            )
            return all_papers
        except requests.exceptions.HTTPError:
            return all_papers
        except Exception as e:
            logger.warning("scopus: %s", e)
            return all_papers
@

\subsection{Applying filters}

Scopus supports rich query syntax where filters are appended to the
search query as Boolean clauses.
This contrasts with IEEE Xplore, which uses dedicated API parameters
for each filter.

The supported filter mappings:
\begin{description}
\item[year] [[PUBYEAR = 2020]] or
  [[PUBYEAR > 2019 AND PUBYEAR < 2025]].
\item[open access] [[OPENACCESS(1)]].
\item[venue] [[SRCTITLE(journal name)]].
\item[pub types] [[DOCTYPE(ar)]] for articles,
  [[DOCTYPE(cp)]] for conference papers,
  [[DOCTYPE(re)]] for reviews, [[DOCTYPE(bk)]] for books.
\item[min citations] Not supported natively---we warn and
  ignore.
\end{description}

<<scopus apply filters>>=
if filters:
    clauses = []

    if filters.year:
        start_year, end_year = filters.year_range()
        if start_year and end_year:
            if start_year == end_year:
                clauses.append(
                    f"PUBYEAR = {start_year}"
                )
            else:
                clauses.append(
                    f"PUBYEAR > {start_year - 1} "
                    f"AND PUBYEAR < {end_year + 1}"
                )
        elif start_year:
            clauses.append(
                f"PUBYEAR > {start_year - 1}"
            )
        elif end_year:
            clauses.append(
                f"PUBYEAR < {end_year + 1}"
            )

    if filters.open_access:
        clauses.append("OPENACCESS(1)")

    if filters.venue:
        clauses.append(f"SRCTITLE({filters.venue})")

    if filters.pub_types:
        type_mapping = {
            "article": "ar",
            "conference": "cp",
            "review": "re",
            "book": "bk",
        }
        doc_types = []
        for pt in filters.pub_types:
            mapped = type_mapping.get(pt.lower())
            if mapped:
                doc_types.append(mapped)
            else:
                logger.warning(
                    "scopus: Publication type '%s' "
                    "not supported, ignoring",
                    pt,
                )
        if doc_types:
            dtype_clause = " OR ".join(
                f"DOCTYPE({dt})" for dt in doc_types
            )
            clauses.append(f"({dtype_clause})")

    if filters.min_citations is not None:
        logger.warning(
            "scopus: Citation count filter "
            "not supported, ignoring"
        )

    if clauses:
        search_query = (
            f"({query}) AND "
            + " AND ".join(clauses)
        )
@

\subsection{Converting entry format}

The Scopus Search API returns entries with Dublin Core
([[dc:]]) and PRISM ([[prism:]]) fields.
Author keywords ([[authkeywords]]) are a pipe-delimited string that
we split into our [[keywords]] field
(see \cref{sec:providers-keyword-mapping}).

<<convert scopus entry>>=
def _convert_entry(self, entry: dict) -> Paper:
    """Convert a Scopus search entry to a Paper."""
    year = None
    cover_date = entry.get("prism:coverDate")
    if cover_date:
        try:
            year = int(cover_date[:4])
        except (ValueError, TypeError):
            pass

    authors = []
    creator = entry.get("dc:creator")
    if creator:
        authors.append(creator)

    url = None
    for link in entry.get("link", []):
        if link.get("@ref") == "scopus":
            url = link.get("@href")
            break

    keywords = None
    auth_kw = entry.get("authkeywords")
    if auth_kw and isinstance(auth_kw, str):
        keywords = [
            kw.strip()
            for kw in auth_kw.split("|")
            if kw.strip()
        ]

    citation_count = None
    cited_by = entry.get("citedby-count")
    if cited_by is not None:
        try:
            citation_count = int(cited_by)
        except (ValueError, TypeError):
            pass

    return Paper(
        title=entry.get("dc:title", "") or "",
        authors=authors,
        year=year,
        doi=entry.get("prism:doi"),
        abstract=entry.get("dc:description"),
        venue=entry.get("prism:publicationName"),
        url=url,
        citation_count=citation_count,
        keywords=keywords,
        sources=[self.name],
    )
